# Clean Code Principles

## Description
General clean code principles to maintain code quality and readability.

## Usage
1. Copy this file to your project's `.cursorrules` or `.cursor/rules/` directory
2. Customize based on your team's coding standards
3. Restart Cursor or reload the workspace

## Rules

```
Code Quality Standards:
- Write self-documenting code with clear variable and function names
- Keep functions small and focused (single responsibility principle)
- Follow DRY (Don't Repeat Yourself) principle
- Add comments only when necessary to explain "why", not "what"
- Maintain consistent code formatting and style
- Use meaningful names that reveal intent
- Avoid magic numbers and strings - use named constants
- Keep nesting levels shallow (max 3-4 levels)
- Write unit tests for critical functionality
- Handle errors gracefully with proper error messages
- Remove dead code and unused imports
- Keep code modular and loosely coupled
```

## Notes
- These principles are language-agnostic
- Adapt to your specific project requirements
- Consider using a linter/formatter for automatic enforcement
